  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA warm front will continue to slowly lift north this afternoon as \nits parent sfc low moves northeast through northern MO and its \nattendant cold front approaches from the west. Shower and storms are \nexpected to begin to redevelop over northwestern portions of the \narea over the next few hours. SPC upgraded to a Slight Risk (level 2 \nof 5) over our northern most counties in southern IL and southwest \nIN for this afternoon. This is based primarily on the potential for \ndestabilization due to daytime heating over the next few hours, and \nexcessively high PWATs that are greater than 2\" causing the \npotential for damaging winds. The main threats this afternoon would \nbe damaging winds and the possibility of an isolated tornado. \nLooking into model soundings there could be a potential for a few \nlow-topped supercells or \"spinny showers\" which could result in the \npossibility of a \"sneaky\" tornado or two. Will need to keep a close \neye on the evolving situation this afternoon. The severe threat \nexists mainly till sunset as conditions wane quickly after that, \nhowever showers may continue for several more hours.\n\nA cold front then moves through the area tonight and brings us a big \ncool down for tomorrow and Saturday. Highs will be 25-30 degrees \ncooler tomorrow as they struggle to reach 60 degrees, and will hover \nright around 60 on Saturday. Winds will be breezy out of the \nnorthwest tomorrow with gusts to around 30 mph at times. Saturday \nnight shows a pretty good potential for a first frost across a \nmajority of the CWA with overnight lows in the 30s. Temperatures \nthen begin to moderate Sunday with sfc high pressure to the \nnortheast and southerly return flow. Expect a gradual warming trend \nthrough the week.",
